.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The navigation entries of a sidebar, in either \fBSidebarStyle\fR. One component, so an app describes its destinations once and the presentation follows the layout.
.SH CONSTRUCTORS
.TP
.B SidebarList::new(items: impl IntoIterator<Item = SidebarItem<M>>)
.SH PROPERTIES
Builder methods, chained after the constructor.
.TP
.B \&.style(style: SidebarStyle)
.TP
.B \&.padding(padding: SidebarPadding)
How roomy the rows are (\fBSidebarPadding\fR): \fBSm\fR dense like Mantine, \fBMd\fR like Nautilus (default), \fBLg\fR like GNOME Settings. An item's own \fBSidebarItem::padding\fR wins.
.TP
.B \&.cards_below(class: crate::breakpoints::WidthClass)
Chooses the style by the width of the list's own container, with the same rule as \fBMetaComponent\fR (as \fBResponsiveTable\fR does): cards below this width class, the compact list from it. Overrides \fBstyle\fR and \fBfollow\fR. The list then fills its container.
.TP
.B \&.window_radius(r: f32)
Window radius for the \fBSidebarStyle::List\fR bottom corners.
.TP
.B \&.follow(state: &crate::adaptive::Adaptive<K>, present: impl Fn(&K) \->(SidebarStyle, SidebarPadding))
Makes the presentation follow the layout an \fBAdaptive\fR shows: \fBpresent\fR maps the shown layout to a style and a padding. The items stay the same in every layout (describe the destinations once); only how they are drawn changes, and it changes together with the layout, never ahead of it. Until a layout is shown, the list keeps its own \fBstyle\fR and \fBpadding\fR.
.PP
.nf
